pseudo-Boolean proof version 3.0
% A checked deletion MAY carry a witness while an order is loaded, and this
% instance pins that. `proof_format_overview.md` used to say the substitution
% has to be empty whenever an order is loaded or strengthening-to-core is on;
% only the strengthening-to-core half is a real side condition, because there
% `DeletionChecker` generates no substituted proofgoals at all. Under a loaded
% order it generates them, including the order's own goals: `delc` calls
% `ActiveOrder::get_proofgoals` exactly as `red` does, so the witness owes
% `O(z|w, z)` -- it may not worsen the order. Banning the witness here would
% reject sound proofs; the twin instance
% `incorrect/version3/checked_deletion_witness_worsens_order.pbp` is the same
% proof with a witness that does worsen the order, and is refused.
%
% The order is `v1 >= u1`, so a witness may only lower x1. `x1 -> 0 x2 -> 1`
% lowers it, and every goal holds: from `1 x3 >= 1` and the negation of the
% deleted `1 x1 1 x2 >= 1` (i.e. x1 = x2 = 0), the witnessed assignment
% (0, 1, 1) satisfies both original constraints and the order goal
% `-0 + x1 >= 0` is trivially true.
f 2;
def_order simple
    vars
        left u1;
        right v1;
        aux;
    end;
    def
        -1 u1 1 v1 >= 0;
    end;
    transitivity
        vars
            fresh_right w1;
        end;
        proof
            proofgoal #1
                pol -2 -3 + -1 +;
            qed : -1;
        qed;
    end;
end;
load_order simple x1;
% Put something in the derived set, so that a failed checked deletion could not
% quietly fall back to unchecked deletion: the acceptance below is the checked
% path and nothing else.
pol 1 2 +;
delc 1 : x1 -> 0 x2 -> 1 ;
output NONE;
conclusion NONE;
end pseudo-Boolean proof;
